<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Riad Al Solh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1894">1894</a>–<a href="/years/1951">1951</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Riad al-Sulh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Riad Al Solh was a Lebanese nationalist politician and the first prime minister of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> Muslim from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, he made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-pact">National Pact</a> of 1943 with President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bechara-el-khoury">Bechara El Khoury</a> and led the struggle that ended the French mandate. He was prime minister in 1943–1945 and 1946–1951 and was assassinated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a>, Jordan, in July 1951.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/riad-al-solh.jpg" alt="Riad Al Solh" width="320" height="555" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Riad-el-Solh.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Riad Al Solh was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a> on 17 August 1894 to Sunni Muslim parents. His father, Reda Al Solh, was vice-governor in Nabatiyyah and in Sidon and a leading Arab nationalist. In 1915 Ottoman forces tried him, and he went into exile in Smyrna. He later served as Minister of the Interior in Emir Faisal's government in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a>. Riad spent most of his youth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>, where his father was a deputy in the Ottoman Parliament, and he studied law and political science at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-paris">University of Paris</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Solh served as prime minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> twice. His first term ran from 25 September 1943 to 10 January 1945, and President Bishara Al Khouri chose him as the first prime minister. In November 1943 the two made the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-pact">National Pact</a>, al Mithaq al Watani, an unwritten gentleman's agreement that gave the offices of president, prime minister and speaker of parliament to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maronites">Maronite Christians</a>, the Sunni Muslims and the Shiite Muslims, on the basis of the 1932 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. During this term he was also Minister of Finance from September 1943 to July 1944 and minister of supplies and reserves from 3 July 1944 to 9 January 1945.</p><p>He held the premiership again from 14 December 1946 to 14 February 1951, again under Khouri. He was critical of King Abdullah, and in his second term he played a significant part in winning the blessing of the political committee of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arab-league">Arab League</a> for the All-Palestine Government. He is regarded as one of the founders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, for he was able to unite the religious groups in the struggle for independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Solh escaped unhurt from an assassination attempt in March 1950 by a member of the Syrian Social Nationalist Party. Several months after he left office he was shot dead on 17 July 1951 at Marka Airport in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a> by three gunmen of the same party, who acted in revenge for the execution of Anton Saadeh, one of its founders.</p><p>He was married to Fayza al-Jabiri, a Syrian and sister of the former Syrian prime minister Saadallah al-Jabiri. They had several daughters and a son, Reda, who died in infancy. Their eldest daughter, Alia, followed his path, and Leila became one of the first two women ministers in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. Patrick Seale's book The Struggle for Arab Independence (2011) focuses on Solh's career, and a square in downtown <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, Riad al-Solh Square, bears his name.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Riad Al Solh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sidon">Sidon</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amman">Amman</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/jordan">Jordan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=33.56,35.38&amp;year=1931" aria-label="Sidon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Riad Al Solh"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5382 4687h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5382 4687h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=33.56,35.38&amp;year=1931">What was here in 1931? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
